# Quality gates for ccmux.
#
# This repo is two stacks and neither is where auto-detection looks: the Go daemon's
# go.mod lives in daemon/, not at the root, and a SwiftPM Package.swift is not a
# marker qa-receipt.sh knows. Without this file the repo detects no test command at
# all and can never record a green.
#
# This file REPLACES auto-detection. Anything not listed here stops being gated, so
# add a line when a stack is added rather than assuming detection will pick it up.
#
# The Swift lines are guarded on the toolchain being present, so on a Linux host
# they SKIP and a green signed there covers the Go daemon only. Guarding beats not
# guarding: `swift build` where there is no swift is not a gate that fails, it is a
# gate that cannot run, and it took the whole receipt down with it — no Go gating
# either, and no green recordable from Linux at all.
#
# Added 2026-08-29: gofmt, a Go complexity ratchet, and a syntax check for the
# web lens. Before them this file held only `go vet` + `go test`, so formatting
# reached /ship's AI review instead of a gate (61789a5 repaired gofmt in two api
# files). The other two are cheap floors under things nothing checked at all
# rather than fixes for a measured miss. All three are ordered before the Go
# gates because they fail in milliseconds.
#
# Read the skip as permanent: development is Linux-only and goes straight to CI,
# so nobody runs these two lines. What that leaves is worth knowing but has not
# been a problem in practice — release.yml's macos-15 job builds the app on every
# v* tag, and that has been catching what it needs to. Recorded as scope, not as
# a defect: a green from this file covers the Go daemon, and Swift is covered by
# the tag build. `swift test` is not run by either.

# --- static checks first: they are the cheapest and fail fastest ---

# Formatting. See .claude/gates/gofmt.sh for why this is a script and not the
# one-liner it replaced: `gofmt -l` exits 2 with EMPTY stdout on a file that does
# not parse, so judging it by the emptiness of its output alone passed on
# unparseable Go.
bash .claude/gates/gofmt.sh

# Web lens syntax. daemon/web/ is four hand-written JS files with no build step,
# no bundler and no test harness, so until this line the only thing standing
# between a mistake in it and a release was review — which does catch things
# (1b06e8f, a stale row count fed to every lens, caught pre-v0.1.45) but is not
# a gate and does not run on every commit. `node --check` is only a parse — it
# catches typos and unbalanced braces, not logic — but that is the floor and it
# costs milliseconds. A lint pass with complexity/length caps is the obvious next
# step and deliberately NOT bundled here: it needs an eslint config and a
# node_modules this repo does not have, which is a bigger decision than a gate.
command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "qa-gates: node not installed — cannot syntax-check daemon/web"; exit 1; }
for f in daemon/web/*.js; do node --check "$f" || exit 1; done

# Go complexity, capped at 10, as a ratchet over frozen pre-existing debt.
# See .claude/gates/gocyclo.sh for why it is a ratchet and not a wall.
bash .claude/gates/gocyclo.sh

# --- then the real work ---
cd daemon && go vet ./...
cd daemon && go test ./...
# Written as if/else, NOT `command -v swift && swift build || echo ...`. That shorter
# form passes the gate whenever `swift build` FAILS, because `||` cannot tell a missing
# toolchain from a broken build. if/else keeps the real exit code when swift is there.
if command -v swift >/dev/null 2>&1; then swift build; else echo "qa-gates: no swift toolchain — SKIPPING swift build (Go gates still ran)"; fi
if command -v swift >/dev/null 2>&1; then swift test; else echo "qa-gates: no swift toolchain — SKIPPING swift test (Go gates still ran)"; fi
